\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 "contextId": "ctx-123"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 This is one of the most important fields to understand.\
A context groups 
\f1\b related interactions/tasks
\f0\b0 .\
Think of it as:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 Context = conversation / business interaction\
\
ctx-123\

\f3 \uc0\u9474 
\f2 \

\f3 \uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472 
\f2  task-001 Search flights\

\f3 \uc0\u9474 
\f2 \

\f3 \uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472 
\f2  task-002 Check baggage\

\f3 \uc0\u9474 
\f2 \

\f3 \uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472 
\f2  task-003 Change booking\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 The server normally creates the context for a new interaction.\
Then subsequent calls can attach to it.\
The current spec says client messages may omit both 
\f2\fs26 contextId
\f0\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 taskId
\f0\fs24 ; if 
\f2\fs26 taskId
\f0\fs24  alone is supplied, the server can infer its corresponding context. If both are supplied, they must agree. Server-originated messages include a context ID. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/definitions/?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 A2A Protocol}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

