\cf0 Exactly. 
\f1\b A2A is not merely a chatbot API with 
\f4\fs26 messageId
\f1\fs24  + 
\f4\fs26 contextId
\f1\fs24 .
\f0\b0  The 
\f2\fs26 Task
\f0\fs24  is the part that makes it an 
\f1\b agent protocol rather than just a conversational messaging protocol
\f0\b0 .\
The cleanest way to understand it is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 Message = "something was said"\
\
Context = "these interactions belong together"\
\
Task = "there is a piece of work being performed"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 A2A explicitly defines a Task as a 
\f1\b stateful unit of work with its own lifecycle
\f0\b0 . An agent may answer a message directly, or it may create a Task when the interaction represents work that needs to be tracked. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/key-concepts/?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 A2A Protocol}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Think about a normal chatbot first\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 With a normal chatbot API:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 User\
  
\f3 \uc0\u9474 
\f2 \
  
\f3 \uc0\u9474 
\f2  "Find me a flight from BLR to London"\
  
\f3 \uc0\u9660 
\f2 \
Chat API\
  
\f3 \uc0\u9474 
\f2 \
  
\f3 \uc0\u9474 
\f2  "Here are the flights..."\
  
\f3 \uc0\u9660 
\f2 \
User\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

